.TH RECENSE 1 "2026-07-31" "senechal" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
recense \- take a census of the executables installed under a home directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B recense
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B recense where
\fIname\fR
.br
.B recense shadowed
.br
.B recense unwired
.br
.B recense paths
.br
.B recense
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B recense
answers one question about one home directory:
.B what can this user invoke that this user installed?
It walks the search path, keeps only the entries that lie under the home
directory, and prints the executable names they provide, one per line, with the
file that provides each.
.PP
The distinction it exists to hold is
.B installed
versus
.BR present .
A home directory contains thousands of executable files \- build trees,
virtualenvs, vendored toolchains, checked\-out repositories \- and almost none
of them are installed. An executable is installed when a directory on the
search path reaches it; everything else is a file that happens to have the
execute bit. A census that walks
.BR find (1)
over a home directory reports the second population under the first
population's name, which is the specific error this verb refuses to make.
.PP
Ownership is not inferred. A name provided by a version manager's shim
directory is reported at the path the shim occupies, not at whatever it
dispatches to, because the dispatch target is a property of the moment and the
shim is the thing that is installed.
.TP
.B (no subcommand)
The census. One line per installed executable:
.RI name , TAB , "absolute path" .
Sorted by name. A name provided by more than one directory appears once, at the
provider the shell chooses; use
.B shadowed
for the ones it does not.
.TP
.B where
Report which home directory provides
.IR name ,
and whether that provider is what the shell actually runs. A name
installed under the home directory but shadowed by a system provider earlier on
the search path is a finding, not a hit, and is printed as such.
.TP
.B shadowed
List the installed names that lose. Each line names the home provider and the
provider that wins. This is the population a caller believes is installed and
is not getting.
.TP
.B unwired
List the executables sitting in a conventional install directory that no search
path entry reaches. The scope is deliberately the install directories \-
.IR ~/bin ,
.IR ~/.local/bin ,
a language toolchain's
.I bin
or
.I shims
directory \- and not the whole home directory. A walk of the whole home
directory returns the
.B present
population: build outputs, virtualenv interpreters, vendored binaries, every
executable in every checkout. None of those were installed and none of them
were meant to be invoked by name, so listing them answers a question
nobody asked with ten thousand lines. An install directory missing from the
search path is the failure this subcommand is looking for, and it is a
one\-line fix.
.TP
.B paths
List the search path entries under the home directory, in search order, with
the count of executables each provides. Duplicated entries are printed as
duplicates rather than collapsed: a path entry listed twice is a shell
configuration defect, and a census that silently deduplicates it hides the
defect it is best placed to see.
.PP
.B recense
reads. It installs nothing, removes nothing, and writes no file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Results on standard output, commentary suppressed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage summary, including the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the utility name and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of flags; everything after is a positional argument.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
kept \- the census was taken.
.TP
.B 2
usage error \- unknown subcommand, or
.B where
called without a name.
.TP
.B 6
blind \- a search path entry under the home directory could not be read, so the
census is incomplete. Nothing is reported as absent on the strength of a
directory that was never seen.
.TP
.B 9
nothing matched \-
.B where
was asked about a name this home does not install, or
.BR shadowed / unwired
found none. Not an error: it is the answer. The shell convention for this is
.BR 1 ,
but the ecosystem reserves every code through
.B 6
for its shared vocabulary, so a seventh meaning is taken above the reserved
range rather than by redefining a code every other verb already uses.
.PP
.B recense
cannot spend money. It carries no
.B \-\-summon
flag and makes no network request; every question it answers is answerable from
the filesystem and the environment.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
The search path the census is taken over.
.TP
.B HOME
The home directory that bounds it. Entries outside it are not the subject.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Asking about a name this home does not install:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ recense where no\-such\-utility
recense: no\-such\-utility is not installed under /home/zach
$ echo $?
9
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ recense \-\-version
recense (bashified)
.fi
.RE
.PP
The remaining forms report the state of one particular machine, so an expected
output here is a fixture rather than a fact and none is written: bare
.B recense
is the census itself,
.B recense paths
lists the search path entries under the home directory in search order,
.B recense shadowed
names the installed executables the shell does not reach, and
.B recense unwired
names the install directories the search path misses.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR which (1),
.BR command (1),
.BR type (1),
.BR compgen (1),
.BR ausculte (1),
.BR veille (1)
.PP
.B recense
is to a home directory what
.BR which (1)
is to one name: the same question, asked of the whole install surface at once,
and answered with the misses as well as the hits.
